import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Retro — spike Q3: which way does the integration point?","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Retro — spike Q3: which way does the integration point?","type":"retro","date":"2026-10-14T00:00:00.000Z"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/retros/handl-q3-integration-direction.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/retros/handl-q3-integration-direction.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/project/retros/handl-q3-integration-direction.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="retro-—-spike-q3-which-way-does-the-integration-point" tabindex="-1">Retro — spike Q3: which way does the integration point? <a class="header-anchor" href="#retro-—-spike-q3-which-way-does-the-integration-point" aria-label="Permalink to “Retro — spike Q3: which way does the integration point?”">​</a></h1><p><strong>Plan</strong>: 0029, <strong>Q3</strong>. <strong>Verdict</strong>: <strong>(A) — catopt embeds handl as an optional peer</strong>, on the measured facts below. (B) is not excluded forever; it is recorded with the condition that would make it viable.</p><h2 id="the-question" tabindex="-1">The question <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-question" aria-label="Permalink to “The question”">​</a></h2><p>Two shapes were on the table:</p><ul><li><strong>(A) catopt embeds handl</strong> — a Rust peer (PyO3 / <code>maturin</code>), catopt calls handl for the language and its rewrite/typecheck engines.</li><li><strong>(B) handl drives catopt</strong> — catopt&#39;s vocabulary is a handl language, tensor ops are host functions registered by a catopt plugin, and catopt&#39;s discovery/RL become handl tools.</li></ul><h2 id="the-measured-facts" tabindex="-1">The measured facts <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-measured-facts" aria-label="Permalink to “The measured facts”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>fact</th><th>source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The <strong>load path is a Rust API</strong>, not a CLI path: <code>language_from_dir</code> reads the ten-file layout; <code>hnl</code> selects kits through <em>registered emitters</em></td><td>Q0; <code>crates/host-cli/src/main.rs</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>FragmentSource::DataFile</code> is modeled but <strong>returns <code>LangError::NotImplemented</code></strong> (&quot;the emitters are the active path&quot;)</td><td><code>crates/handl-kit-data/src/lib.rs</code></td></tr><tr><td>A tensor crosses as <strong><code>ForeignResource</code></strong> — <code>{ptr: *mut c_void, finalizer, dropped}</code>, <code>Rc</code>-held, <strong>not <code>Send</code>/<code>Sync</code></strong>, never dereferenced by handl</td><td>Q1a; <code>crates/handl-value/src/lib.rs</code></td></tr><tr><td>A Rust peer is <strong>already an established shape in catopt</strong>: <code>catopt-native</code> is optional, excluded from the uv workspace, <code>maturin</code>-built, opt-in via <code>engine=</code></td><td><code>AGENTS.md</code>; <code>packages/catopt-native</code></td></tr><tr><td>catopt&#39;s CI installs a CPU torch wheel and expects no Rust toolchain</td><td><code>.github/workflows/ci.yml</code></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="why-a" tabindex="-1">Why (A) <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-a" aria-label="Permalink to “Why (A)”">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>The working path is a Rust-caller API.</strong> Q0 proved a <code>Language</code> assembles from catopt&#39;s data — <em>when a Rust caller invokes <code>language_from_dir</code></em>. (B) would require handl-side work first (implementing runtime data-file kits, or a Python host bridge) before catopt&#39;s vocabulary could even load from the CLI.</li><li><strong>The tensor channel is a raw pointer, which a PyO3 peer produces natively.</strong> (A) hands handl a pointer plus a finalizer that decrefs — exactly the <code>ForeignResource</code> contract. (B) would require handl to embed CPython to reach torch, inverting ownership and dragging a Python runtime into the kernel.</li><li><strong>The precedent and the CI story already exist.</strong> <code>catopt-native</code> is the same shape: an optional Rust peer that the default path does not need. (A) preserves core&#39;s zero-dependency contract and keeps catopt&#39;s CI light; the peer is opt-in.</li><li><strong>The useful integration is the language, not the tensor runtime.</strong> ADR 0011 decides that rewriting is canonical in the calculus and execution stays catopt&#39;s. So the peer&#39;s job is small and well-bounded: load a <code>Language</code> from catopt&#39;s fragments, run rewrite/typecheck passes, hand back terms — no tensors need cross at all in the first version.</li></ol><h2 id="the-seam-test-the-pass-criterion" tabindex="-1">The seam test (the pass criterion) <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-seam-test-the-pass-criterion" aria-label="Permalink to “The seam test (the pass criterion)”">​</a></h2><p>Named, and half-built already:</p><ol><li><strong>Forward</strong> — catopt&#39;s store → fragments → handl <code>Language</code>: <em>exists</em> (<code>catopt_fragments.rs</code>, handl <code>b20b978a</code>).</li><li><strong>Reverse</strong> — a handl term/row → a catopt cell, identity on the shipped bank: <code>cell_from_handl</code> exists in catopt; the <strong>round-trip test is not yet written</strong> and is the seam&#39;s acceptance test.</li><li><strong>No tensors in v1</strong> — the crossing is deliberately value-free, so the unsafe/single-threaded caveats of <code>ForeignResource</code> do not apply yet.</li></ol><h2 id="what-b-would-need-to-become-viable" tabindex="-1">What (B) would need to become viable <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-b-would-need-to-become-viable" aria-label="Permalink to “What (B) would need to become viable”">​</a></h2><ul><li>handl implements <strong>runtime data-file kits</strong> (<code>FragmentSource::DataFile</code> stops returning <code>NotImplemented</code>), so a language is loadable without a Rust caller; and</li><li>a <strong>host bridge</strong> so handl can call tensor primitives that live in Python/torch (PyO3 embedding, or an FFI to a tensor library).</li></ul><p>If both land, (B) is the more ambitious framing — catopt as an <em>application of the platform</em> rather than a project with a peer — and this decision should be revisited then. It is not a rejection, it is a sequencing.</p><h2 id="honest-caveats" tabindex="-1">Honest caveats <a class="header-anchor" href="#honest-caveats" aria-label="Permalink to “Honest caveats”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>(A) still costs a second toolchain</strong>, optionally. The mitigation is the <code>catopt-native</code> pattern: excluded from the workspace, built with <code>maturin</code>, opt-in. If that pattern ever becomes load-bearing for the default path, the cost is real.</li><li><strong>The peer is young.</strong> handl&#39;s own guide notes a ~8.5 GB debug target and memory-hungry test binaries; a catopt-side CI job that builds it must respect that (<code>CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG=0</code>, <code>-j 2</code>).</li><li><strong>Nothing here is validated by a tensor run.</strong> Q1b (a resource crossing through exec) is still unmeasured; this decision does not depend on it, but the &quot;host functions&quot; claim in ADR 0010 does.</li></ul>`,17)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};